# /sim/set_external_wrench — hang a KNOWN external wrench on a simulated body,
# or take it off.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The momentum observer and the payload estimator (#135) can
# only be validated against a load whose true value is known, and the simulator
# had no way to produce one: MuJoCoSimulator::SetExternalForce was reachable
# from tests only, so every sim measurement of the estimator lane was taken at
# zero load and had zero variance by construction (#135 D16). This service is
# the positive control that breaks that degeneracy.
#
# THE WRENCH ACTS AT `point`, NOT AT THE BODY CENTRE OF MASS. This distinction
# is the whole contract and it is not cosmetic: MuJoCo's mjData::xfrc_applied
# is applied at the body's CoM (`xipos`), which on a real model sits centimetres
# away from the body frame origin — 35 mm for `ee_link` on iiwa7_leap, and
# non-zero on EVERY body of that model, massless ones included. Forwarding a
# pure force there would silently add a moment (0.25 N.m at 10 N, five times the
# estimator's noise floor) and read back as a payload with a centre-of-mass
# offset that does not exist. The simulator therefore converts the request into
# the CoM-referenced equivalent every tick, so a wrench named "at ee_link" means
# the same thing here as it does to the pinocchio frame of that name — their
# origins were measured to coincide.
#
# THE WRENCH IS LATCHED, NOT AN IMPULSE. It is re-applied every physics tick
# until it is cleared, a zero wrench replaces it, or the simulator is reset. One
# call is enough to hang a load; there is no need to republish it.

# Body to apply to, as named in the MJCF. REQUIRED unless `clear` is true.
# A name the model does not carry is REFUSED (accepted=false) rather than
# ignored — a silently dropped request would leave the caller believing a load
# is hanging while the estimator correctly reports nothing, which is the exact
# failure this whole lane was built to make impossible.
string body_name

# Point the wrench acts at, in `body_name`'s LOCAL frame [m]. Zero — the
# default for an unset field — is the body frame origin, which is what you want
# whenever the wrench is meant to line up with a model frame of the same name.
# A non-zero value hangs the load off a known offset, e.g. a mass held 10 cm
# below the palm, without needing a body to exist at that spot.
geometry_msgs/Vector3 point

# Wrench about `point`, expressed in WORLD-frame axes [N, N.m]. It is the
# wrench the environment applies TO the robot, matching the sign convention
# PayloadEstimator inherits from the momentum observer: a hanging mass m is
# force = m * g, pointing DOWN.
geometry_msgs/Wrench wrench

# True removes EVERY staged wrench in the scene, not just `body_name`'s, and
# `body_name`/`point`/`wrench` are then ignored. To remove one body's load
# while others stay, send that body a zero `wrench` instead.
bool clear

---

# True iff the request was applied. False means nothing changed — the body name
# was unknown or empty, or the simulator was not running.
bool accepted

# Human-readable outcome, including the resolved body id on success and the
# reason for the refusal on failure.
string message
